  data: {
    hero: {
      title: 'FAPFAP',
      subtitle: 'The Last Trick Counts',
    },
    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Fapfap',
            subtitle: 'The Last Trick Counts',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Fapfap, known as Agram in Niger and Mali, is a simple yet strategic last-trick-only game from West Africa. Players compete to win the final trick after five rounds of play, with the unusual twist that winning earlier tricks can be a disadvantage as it forces you to lead and cede control. A stripped 35-card deck is used, with unique ranking and the removal of the Ace of Spades.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Originating in the Sahel region of West Africa, Fapfap evolved from European trick-taking games introduced during the colonial era. The game is known by different names across the region: Agram in Niger and Mali, Sink-Sink in parts of Niger, and Fapfap in Cameroon. It is closely related to Sparrow in Ghana.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '2 to 5 players.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '35-card stripped deck. Take a standard 52-card deck and remove: all Kings, all Queens, all Jacks, all 2s, and the Ace of Spades (The Chief). Remaining deck: Ace (except Spades), 3-10 of all suits.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Deck and chips/stakes for optional scoring variant.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Dealer (rotates clockwise) shuffles; right-hand player cuts. Dealer distributes 5 or 6 cards to each player (depending on regional variant) in two batches. Cameroon: 6 cards (3, then 3). Niger: 5 cards (3, then 2).',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Agram (Niger): Standard version with 6 cards dealt (3, then 3). Winner of 6th trick wins.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Sink-Sink (Niger): Identical to Agram except only 5 cards dealt (3, then 2). Winner of 5th trick wins.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: "Fapfap (Cameroon): 5 or 6 cards dealt. Includes scoring multipliers: winning final trick with a 3 = double stake; two consecutive tricks with 3's = quadruple stake.",
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Agram (Mali): 31-card deck (removes 6-2 of all suits, plus Ace of Spades). Unusual rank order: A-K-10-Q-9-8-J-7. 5 cards dealt (2, then 3).',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Sparrow (Ghana): Related game with different deck composition and regional rule modifications.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
    description: 'Fapfap, known as Agram in Niger and Mali, is a simple yet strategic last-trick-only game from West Africa. Players compete to win the final trick after five rounds of play, with the unusual twist that winning earlier tricks can be a disadvantage as it forces you to lead and cede control. A stripped 35-card deck is used, with unique ranking and the removal of the Ace of Spades.',
