    historyContent: {
      origins: 'Originating in the Sahel region of West Africa, Fapfap evolved from European trick-taking games introduced during the colonial era. The game is known by different names across the region: Agram in Niger and Mali, Sink-Sink in parts of Niger, and Fapfap in Cameroon. It is closely related to Sparrow in Ghana.',
      originCountries: [
        'Cameroon',
        'Niger',
        'Mali',
      ],
      timeline: [
        'Colonial Era: European trick-taking mechanics adapted by West African populations with regional card removals and rules modifications.',
        '20th Century: Established as a staple social game throughout the Sahel, played in cafes and street gatherings.',
        'Modern Era: Popular in Cameroon and across West Africa; remains largely unknown in Western card gaming circles.',
      ],
      evolution: 'Regional variants developed unique deck compositions and scoring systems. The Cameroon version (Fapfap) emphasizes the final trick with multiplier bonuses for winning with low cards (3s, 4s).',
      cultural: 'Played as a fast-paced social game, often for small monetary stakes or social prestige. Traditionally played in informal social settings and commercial gatherings.',
    },
