          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win the final (5th or 6th) trick. Winning earlier tricks may be disadvantageous as the trick winner must lead the next trick.',
          gameplay: "1. **Starting Play**: The player to the dealer's right leads the first trick with any card. 2. **Following Suit**: Players must follow the suit of the led card if able. If unable, any card may be played. 3. **Winning a Trick**: The highest card of the led suit wins the trick. No trump suit exists. 4. **Next Lead**: The winner of the trick leads the next trick with any card. 5. **Final Trick**: After 5 or 6 tricks (depending on variant), the winner of the last trick wins the game. 6. **Round Completion**: After each complete game, the deal passes to the next player clockwise.",
          keyRules: [
            'Card rank order (high to low): Ace, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3 (except Spades: 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3 only; no Ace of Spades).',
            'Must follow suit if able; otherwise, play any card.',
            'Only the final trick matters; earlier trick wins are often strategic disadvantages.',
            'No trump suit in standard play.',
            'Trick winner leads the next trick, forcing them to potentially give away control.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: 'Avoid winning early tricks if possible. Sluff (discard) low-to-mid range cards early to preserve high cards (Aces, 10s) for the final trick. If you must win, do so with the lowest possible card.',
          intermediate: 'The 3 Strategy: The 3 is the weakest card but carries a multiplier bonus in Cameroon variant. Winning the final trick with a 3 is more valuable than with higher cards because of the stake multiplier, though it requires precise control of previous tricks.',
          advanced: 'Suit Tracking: Memorize which high cards (particularly Aces and 10s) have been played in each suit. Position yourself to lead the final trick with a card that others cannot beat with their remaining cards. Force opponents into wasteful high-card plays early by leading their strong suits.',
          tips: [
            'Never waste an Ace or 10 winning an early trick unless absolutely necessary.',
            'If you win the 4th or 5th trick with a low card, you control the lead for the final trick and can play your strongest card.',
            "Count remaining players' likely hand strength: after 4 tricks, estimate what each opponent still holds.",
            "In Cameroon stakes variant, taking two consecutive tricks (especially the last two) with 3's is the ultimate combo.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
